"Expert Council on Well-Being and AI | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/expert-council-on-well-being-and-ai)\nciteturn2783view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2779view1\",\"lineno\":49}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/index/expert-council-on-well-being-and-ai/; Total lines: 156\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: October 14, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company cite4†Safety L16: # Expert Council on Well-Being and AI\nL17: \nL18: Introducing the members of the Expert Council on Well-Being and AI and how we’ll work together.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Meet the council members\nL25: \nL26:   * cite5†Meet the council members  L27:   * cite6†How we will work with the council members  L28:   * cite7†Expanding our safety work  L29: \nL30:   * cite5†Meet the council members  L31:   * cite6†How we will work with the council members  L32:   * cite7†Expanding our safety work  L33: We’ve assembled the Expert Council on Well-Being and AI to help guide our ongoing work to cite8†build more helpful ChatGPT and Sora experiences for everyone . The council brings together leading researchers and experts with decades of experience studying how technology affects our emotions, motivation, and mental health. Their role is to advise us, pose questions and help define what healthy interactions with AI should look like for all ages.\nL34: Earlier this year, we began consulting many of these experts informally, such as when we were developing cite9†parental controls⁠(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com and the notification language for parents when a teen may be in distress. As we formalized the new council, we broadened our search to include additional experts in psychology, psychiatry, and human-computer interaction, bringing in new perspectives on how people relate to and are affected by technology.\nL35: Because teens use ChatGPT differently than adults, we’ve also included several council members with backgrounds in understanding how to build technology that supports healthy youth development.\nL36: We remain responsible for the decisions we make, but we’ll continue learning from this council, the Global Physician Network, policymakers, and more, as we build advanced AI systems in ways that support people’s well-being.\nL37: ## Meet the council members\nL38: \nL39: We’re grateful to this initial group for their deep expertise and shared commitment to making AI supportive and safe.\nL40: \nL41:   * David Bickham, Ph.D.—Research Director at the Digital Wellness Lab at Boston Children’s Hospital and Assistant Professor at Harvard Medical School. His work looks at how young people’s social media use affects their mental health and development.\nL42:   * Mathilde Cerioli, Ph.D.—Chief Scientific Officer at everyone.AI, a nonprofit helping people understand the opportunities and risks of AI for children. With a Ph.D. in Cognitive Neuroscience and a Master’s Degree in Psychology, her research focuses on how AI intersects with child cognitive and emotional development.\nL43:   * Munmun De Choudhury, Ph.D.—J. Z. Liang Professor of Interactive Computing at Georgia Tech. She harnesses computational approaches to better understand the role of online technologies in shaping and improving mental health.\nL44: \nL45:   * Tracy Dennis-Tiwary, Ph.D.—Professor of Psychology at Hunter College and co-founder and CSO at Arcade Therapeutics. She creates digital games for mental health and explores interactions between technology and emotional wellbeing.\nL46:   * David Mohr, Ph.D.—Professor at Northwestern University and Director of the Center for Behavioral Intervention Technologies. He studies how technology can help prevent and treat common mental health conditions such as depression and anxiety.\nL47: \nL48:   * Robert K. Ross, M.D.—A national leader and expert in health philanthropy, public health, and community-based health initiatives. He began his career as a pediatrician and is formerly the president and CEO of The California Endowment.\nL49: ## How we will work with the council members\nL50: \nL51: We kicked off this council last week with an in-person session to deep dive into OpenAI’s current work in these areas and to have council members meet the teams they will be working with and advising.\nL52: Our work with the council will include regular check-ins on our approach, and recurring meetings to explore topics like how AI should behave in complex or sensitive situations and what kinds of guardrails can best support people using ChatGPT. As an example, for the rollout of cite10†parental controls , we consulted individual members to help us prioritize which controls to build first and how best to notify parents if their teen appears to be in distress.\nL53: Their feedback shaped the tone of the messages we use, so they feel caring and respectful to both teens and the family members.\nL54: The council will also help us think about how ChatGPT can have a positive impact on people’s lives and contribute to their well-being. Some of our initial discussions have focused around what constitutes well-being and the ways ChatGPT might empower people as they navigate all aspects of their life. We’ll keep listening, learning, and sharing what comes out of this work.\nL55: ## Expanding our safety work\nL56: Alongside the Expert Council on Well-Being and AI advising on our broader approach to well-being, we’re also working with a multidisciplinary subset of mental health clinicians and researchers within the cite8†Global Physician Network to shape our model behavior and policies, and to test how ChatGPT responds in real-world situations. This work spans psychiatry, psychology, pediatrics, and crisis intervention, helping ensure our systems are grounded in clinical understanding and best practices.\nL57: We’ll have more to share soon about the cite11†improvements underway⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com to the main ChatGPT model and what we’re learning about how it can best support people.\nL58: \nL59:   * cite12†ChatGPT L60:   * cite13†2025 L61: ## Author\nL62: \nL63: OpenAI\nL64: ## Keep reading\nL65: \nL66: cite14†View all L67: \nL68: cite10†Introducing parental controls ProductSep 29, 2025 L69: \nL70: cite15†Teen safety, freedom, and privacy SafetySep 16, 2025 L71: \nL72: cite16†Building towards age prediction SafetySep 16, 2025 L73: \nL74: Research\nL75: \nL76:   * cite17†Research Index L77:   * cite18†Research Overview L78:   * cite19†Economic Research L79: \nL80: Latest Advancements\nL81: \nL82:   * cite20†GPT-6.1 Sol L83:   * cite21†GPT-6 Astra L84:   * cite22†GPT-5.6 L85:   * cite23†GPT-5.5 L86: \nL87: Safety\nL88:   * cite24†Safety Approach L89:   * cite25†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L90:   * cite26†Security & Privacy L91:   * cite27†Trust & Transparency L92: \nL93: Products\nL94:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L95:   * cite28†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L96:   * cite29†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L97:   * cite30†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L98:   * cite31†Codex†chatgpt.com L99:   * cite32†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L100:   * cite33†Release Notes L101: \nL102: API Platform\nL103: \nL104:   * cite34†Overview L105:   * cite35†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L106:   * cite36†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L107: \nL108: Business\nL109:   * cite37†Overview L110:   * cite38†Solutions L111:   * cite39†Resources L112:   * cite40†Plugins L113:   * cite41†Customer Stories L114:   * cite42†Partner Network L115:   * cite43†Contact Sales L116: \nL117: Developers\nL118: \nL119:   * cite44†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L120:   * cite45†Open Models L121:   * cite46†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L122:   * cite47†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L123:   * cite48†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L124: \nL125: Company\nL126:   * cite49†About Us L127:   * cite50†Our Charter L128:   * cite51†Careers L129:   * cite14†News L130: \nL131: Support\nL132: \nL133:   * cite52†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L134: \nL135: More\nL136: \nL137:   * cite53†Stories L138:   * cite54†Academy L139:   * cite55†Supply Co. L140:   * cite56†Livestreams L141:   * cite57†Podcast L142:   * cite58†RSS L143: \nL144: Terms & Policies\nL145: \nL146:   * cite59†Terms of Use L147:   * cite60†Privacy Policy L148:   * cite61†Other Policies L149: cite62†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite68†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L150: \nL151: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL152: \nL153: English United States\nL154: \nL155: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArgentina’s AI opportunity | OpenAI (https://openai.com/global-affairs/argentinas-ai-opportunity)\nciteturn2783view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2779view2\",\"lineno\":13}); Redirected to URL: https://openai.com/global-affairs/argentinas-ai-opportunity/; Total lines: 131\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: October 14, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Global Affairs L16: # Argentina’s AI opportunity\nL17: \nL18: Collaboration between Sur Energy and OpenAI hopes to bring first Stargate project to Latin America.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Argentina has great potential to host the first Stargate project in Latin America. Powered by clean energy, this landmark infrastructure initiative would help position the country as a regional and global leader in artificial intelligence, driving economic growth and expanding opportunity across the country.\nL25: The project is being led by Sur Energy, building on productive discussions last week in Buenos Aires between President Milei, cabinet officials of his government, and an OpenAI delegation led by Chief Global Affairs Officer Chris Lehane. OpenAI and Sur Energy signed a Letter of Intent (LOI) to explore a large-scale data center project in Argentina. Sur Energy would be the energy and infrastructure developer and lead the formation of a consortium, including partnering with a cloud infrastructure developer.\nL26: Sur Energy plans to ensure that the data-center ecosystem is powered by secure, efficient, and sustainable sources. OpenAI welcomes the potential opportunity to become an offtaker.\nL27: Argentina is entering the AI era with real momentum. Millions of Argentinians use ChatGPT each week, cite4†with adoption more than tripling in the past year⁠(opens in a new window)†cdn.openai.com and highest among young adults (18–34). Argentina also ranks among the top Latin American countries for developers building on OpenAI’s tools: evidence of a country not just using AI, but shaping it.\nL28: This momentum, paired with a growing developer ecosystem, makes Argentina a natural place to build the infrastructure that can unlock its full AI potential.\nL29: In addition to developing the infrastructure needed to power AI, we are discussing opportunities with the Argentinian government to drive AI adoption across Argentina, as part of our cite5†OpenAI for Countries initiative, starting within the government itself.\nL30: As we have seen through similar partnerships around the world, ChatGPT has the potential to assist Argentine government employees, as well as administration and research institutions accelerate their daily work—ultimately saving costs while delivering better services to Argentinian citizens.\nL31: “This milestone is about more than just infrastructure, it’s about putting AI into the hands of more people across Argentina. We’re excited to work with Argentina as it builds toward becoming an AI hub for all of Latin America. President Milei’s vision for how AI can drive Argentina’s growth and creativity is unmistakable and strong. Stargate Argentina will help bring that vision to life.” —Sam Altman, CEO of OpenAI.\nL32: “The Stargate Argentina project represents a historic opportunity for the country. It combines our unique renewable energy potential with the development of critical infrastructure for artificial intelligence on a global scale.\nL33: This alliance turns Argentina into a relevant player in the world’s new digital and energy map, creating quality jobs, attracting international investment, and demonstrating that innovation and energy can be complementary engines of sustainable development.” —Emiliano Kargieman, partner of Sur Energy.\nL34:   * cite6†2025 L35:   * cite7†Partnerships L36: ## Author\nL37: \nL38: OpenAI\nL39: ## Keep reading\nL40: \nL41: cite8†View all L42: \nL43: cite9†Helping small businesses put AI to work Global AffairsSep 30, 2026 L44: \nL45: cite10†OpenAI extends cyber access to Ukraine for civilian defense Global AffairsSep 23, 2026 L46: \nL47: cite11†Sam Altman’s remarks at the United Nations Security Council Global AffairsSep 23, 2026 L48: \nL49: Research\nL50: \nL51:   * cite12†Research Index L52:   * cite13†Research Overview L53:   * cite14†Economic Research L54: \nL55: Latest Advancements\nL56: \nL57:   * cite15†GPT-6.1 Sol L58:   * cite16†GPT-6 Astra L59:   * cite17†GPT-5.6 L60:   * cite18†GPT-5.5 L61: \nL62: Safety\nL63:   * cite19†Safety Approach L64:   * cite20†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L65:   * cite21†Security & Privacy L66:   * cite22†Trust & Transparency L67: \nL68: Products\nL69:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L70:   * cite23†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L71:   * cite24†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L72:   * cite25†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L73:   * cite26†Codex†chatgpt.com L74:   * cite27†Dots(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L75:   * cite28†Release Notes L76: \nL77: API Platform\nL78: \nL79:   * cite29†Overview L80:   * cite30†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L81:   * cite31†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L82: \nL83: Business\nL84:   * cite32†Overview L85:   * cite33†Solutions L86:   * cite34†Resources L87:   * cite35†Plugins L88:   * cite36†Customer Stories L89:   * cite37†Partner Network L90:   * cite38†Contact Sales L91: \nL92: Developers\nL93: \nL94:   * cite39†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L95:   * cite40†Open Models L96:   * cite41†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L97:   * cite42†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L98:   * cite43†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L99: \nL100: Company\nL101:   * cite44†About Us L102:   * cite45†Our Charter L103:   * cite46†Careers L104:   * cite8†News L105: \nL106: Support\nL107: \nL108:   * cite47†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L109: \nL110: More\nL111: \nL112:   * cite48†Stories L113:   * cite49†Academy L114:   * cite50†Supply Co. L115:   * cite51†Livestreams L116:   * cite52†Podcast L117:   * cite53†RSS L118: \nL119: Terms & Policies\nL120: \nL121:   * cite54†Terms of Use L122:   * cite55†Privacy Policy L123:   * cite56†Other Policies L124: cite57†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite58†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite61†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L125: \nL126: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Your privacy choices\nL127: \nL128: English United States\nL129: \nL130: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPreparing for AI's economic impact \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/research/economic-policy-responses)\nciteturn2783view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/research/economic-policy-responses\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 103\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10: Policy\nL11: # Preparing for AI’s economic impact: exploring policy responses\nL12: \nL13: Oct 14, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite5†Image: Preparing for AI’s economic impact: exploring policy responses L16: How will the arrival of cite6†powerful AI systems change the structure of the economy? We are uncertain, and so are external experts. But as AI systems continue to improve, and are adopted at an ever-larger scale, it’s crucial there is more discussion about the tools policymakers could use to respond to AI's economic impacts—whatever their nature. To help with this, we’re sharing several economic policy ideas that merit further study.\nL17: Since launching the cite7†Anthropic Economic Index , we've observed an important shift in AI use. Users are becoming cite8†increasingly likely to delegate full tasks to Claude , “collaborating” with Claude less. As AI models continue to work independently for longer periods of time, and as more employers adopt AI to improve their productivity, we expect this trend to accelerate. The implications for the workforce are uncertain.\nL18: How should policymakers respond? This is not an easy question, nor is it one that any single actor can answer. There is great uncertainty about the scale of the transition ahead, and a wide range of views about how to manage it. But it is imperative to begin formulating ideas now for the economic scenarios we might find ourselves in.\nL19: Over the past year, we’ve worked with economists and policy experts from around the world (including members of our cite9†Economic Advisory Council and participants in our cite10†first Economic Futures Symposium†mccourt.georgetown.edu ) to move this discussion forward. To generate a broad range of ideas, we’ve engaged with both non-partisan thinkers and those from across the political spectrum.\nL20: Below, we briefly explore nine of these categories of ideas, covering workforce development, permitting reform, fiscal policy, and social services.\nL21: \nL22: While we don’t know what the optimal policies will prove to be, we’re committed to sharing ideas in the open, and to being transparent about the economic effects of advanced AI.\nL23: ## Matching policies to scenarios\nL24: \nL25: The rate, scale, and form of AI's economic effects will determine the policy responses that are necessary across the world. Accordingly, we've organized these initial ideas into three broad categories:\nL26: Policy ideas for nearly all scenarios, including those where negative effects on the labor market remain modest. These are policies that their advocates argue merit consideration almost regardless of how significant the disruption of AI proves to be. Given this, many of these proposals have been suggested in other contexts before.\nL27: They include upskilling workers and students for emerging jobs, and reforming permitting processes to enable the construction of energy and computing infrastructure to improve productivity.\nL28: Policy ideas for scenarios with moderate acceleration, where AI leads to measurable wage declines and job losses for large portions of the workforce. Here, more substantial fiscal support for displaced workers might be needed. To cite11†offset negative externalities†www.nber.org imposed on displaced workers from rapid automation, taxes on automation might be considered in this scenario.\nL29: Policy ideas for faster-moving scenarios, potentially involving dramatic job losses and worsening inequality. These proposals are much more ambitious, and are designed to respond to a starkly different economic picture. So far, ideas include using sovereign wealth funds to give citizens stakes in AI revenues, and finding new ways to generate government revenue.\nL30: The proposals below don’t necessarily represent Anthropic's own policy positions. But we’re excited by the breadth of proposals we’ve received, and we hope they encourage further research and debate.\nL31: ## Policies for nearly all scenarios\nL32: ### 1. Invest in upskilling through workforce training grants\nL33: \nL34: At our DC Symposium, Abigail Ball, Executive Director of cite12†American Compass†americancompass.org , presented the cite13†Workforce Training Grant†americancompass.org —a proposal she developed with colleague Oren Cass to direct public resources toward on-the-job training.\nL35: Under this model, governments would provide substantial annual subsidies (Ball and Cass suggest $10,000 per year in the US) directly to employers who create formal trainee positions with structured training programs. This training could take multiple forms: programs operated by individual employers, by employer consortia or industry associations, through partnerships between employers and organized labor, or by technical schools and community colleges working alongside employers.\nL36: American Compass proposes redirecting existing higher education subsidies to fund this program. But a range of other funding mechanisms might also deserve consideration—including the possibility of using taxes on AI consumption to support workforce development initiatives.\nL37: ### 2. Reform tax incentives for worker retention and retraining\nL38: \nL39: Tax policy can, on the margin, incentivize employers to retrain and retain employees rather than reducing headcount.\nL40: Revana Sharfuddin of the cite14†Mercatus Center†www.mercatus.org argues that the US tax code creates a bias favoring physical capital investment over human capital investment. Businesses can immediately expense AI systems through bonus depreciation, yet face numerous restrictions when deducting worker training costs.\nL41: She proposes reforms to the Internal Revenue Code, including eliminating the $5,250 cap on tax-free educational assistance, and extending full and immediate expensing to all job-related training.\nL42: These changes would aim to reduce the cost of retraining relative to the cost of layoffs, helping those workers whose positions might otherwise be on the margins of workforce reduction decisions.\nL43: ### 3. Close corporate tax loopholes\nL44: \nL45: Tax policy expert cite15†David Gamage†law.missouri.edu has outlined reforms designed to prevent AI transformation from straining government budgets. Several of his proposals involve closing the “partnership gap” that allows large businesses to avoid entity-level taxes, and modernizing tax allocation to combat profit shifting and cite16†better capture value†www.taxnotes.com from digital and intangibles-based business models.\nL46: The second reform would allocate business taxes based on customer locations through market-based apportionment, while requiring worldwide combined reporting to treat multinationals and subsidiaries as single entities. This approach is designed to limit artificial profit-shifting to tax havens—a practice that could become more prevalent as AI potentially further increases the economic importance of profits derived from intangibles.\nL47: Gamage argues that \"governments that act first will solve their fiscal challenges and better position residents to thrive in an AI economy. Those that wait will face resource constraints when flexibility is most needed.\"\nL48: ### 4. Accelerate permits and approvals for AI infrastructure\nL49: Anthropic has consistently advocated for cite17†reforming permitting and power procurement processes in the United States and allied nations. Accelerating these processes is needed to develop the infrastructure to train and deploy frontier AI—that is, large-scale data centers, transmission infrastructure, and power generation facilities. Reforms will also unlock investment, economic growth, and job creation in the places where AI is built.\nL50: Failing to accelerate AI infrastructure development will slow productivity and job growth, and it could introduce national security risks from vital AI infrastructure moving offshore.\nL51: Three overlapping sets of U.S. regulatory processes delay building large-scale AI infrastructure for years. The first category is permits. These include a series of land use and environmental approvals at the federal, state, and local levels. Second, state regulatory reviews for transmission projects can cause buildouts of new lines to last 10 years or more. Finally, approvals to interconnect facilities to the electric grid typically take 4-6 years for generation resources.\nL52: Concrete steps to address these challenges include reforms to the National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA), which requires federal agencies to review many projects’ environmental effects. Advance analyses of certain kinds of facilities, such as data centers, could help speed reviews of future projects. Other reforms could include leveraging federal authorities to expedite critical transmission buildouts and upgrades and collaboration with utilities to identify opportunities for fast interconnections.\nL53: As cite18†Tyler Cowen†www.mercatus.org , faculty director of the Mercatus Center and member of our Economic Advisory Council, notes: \"I am all for permitting reform—the energy sector included.\"\nL54: ## Policy ideas for moderate scenarios\nL55: ### 5. Establish trade adjustment assistance for AI displacement\nL56: Several economists are exploring how the cite19†Trade Adjustment Assistance (TAA) model†www.dol.gov – in which affected workers are given opportunities to obtain new skills, or receive other support – might be adapted for labor market disruptions in an era of powerful AI. cite20†Ioana Marinescu†sp2.upenn.edu of the University of Pennsylvania, a member of our Economic Advisory Council, views TAA-like \"AI insurance\" as a mechanism \"to support those who lose jobs due to AI.\"\nL57: Along these lines, cite21†Suchet Mittal†www.linkedin.com and cite22†Sam Manning†scholar.google.com have outlined a potential Automation Adjustment Assistance (AAA) program. They describe how funding AAA at levels similar to TAA—approximately $700 million annually—could be an initial option, with mechanisms built in to increase or decrease the size of the program in line with the pace and scale of AI-driven displacement.\nL58: Mittal and Manning note that if such a program needed to expand in the future, it could potentially be funded through taxes on AI-driven revenues from firms above a certain high level of market capitalization, creating a direct mechanism for the AI sector to support workers displaced by the technology.\nL59: ### 6. Implement taxes on compute or token generation\nL60: \nL61: University of Virginia economists Lee Lockwood and Anton Korinek (a member of our cite9†Economic Advisory Council ) propose cite23†studying of a range of taxes†www.nber.org on “token generation, robots, robot services, and digital services.”\nL62: These taxes offer different potential benefits – and distortionary risks – depending on the stage of AI’s development within the economy. A tax on AI-generated tokens sold to end users (a “token tax”) might be desirable when humans remain dominant consumers in the economy, even if powerful AI reduces the relative economic role of labor.\nL63: Korinek and Lockwood argue that, if the economy reaches a stage where powerful AI systems become themselves major consumers of the economy’s resources, taxing AI resource accumulation–e.g. via taxes on compute and other hardware–might be more effective than token taxes on human end users.\nL64: Although these taxes on computational resources distort investment along an AI-transformed economy’s trajectory, they could become the only remaining mechanism to capture some of the windfall generated by AI if the role of both labor markets and human consumption in the economy declines.\nL65: We believe taxes in this broader category deserve serious study, even though they would directly impact Anthropic's revenue and profitability. These taxes could provide crucial revenue for vital fiscal programs—including several others discussed in this post.\nL66: ## Policy ideas for fast-moving scenarios\nL67: ### 7. Create national sovereign wealth funds with stakes in AI\nL68: \nL69: A growing set of proposals aims to give citizens and governments greater stakes in AI's economic returns. Sovereign wealth funds could enable states to acquire positions in AI-related assets. In scenarios where the AI sector captures an outsized share of economic wealth, government investment could both shape the sector's behavior and \"cite24†distribute AI-derived wealth more equitably†papers.ssrn.com .\"\nL70: Writing for the Centre for British Progress, Emma Casey, Emma Rockall, and Helena Roy have proposed a related concept for the United Kingdom: cite25†an AI Bond†britishprogress.org . The AI Bond would aim to ensure adequate investment in \"the AI stack\" to capture AI's benefits and then distribute its returns more evenly across Britain—even as AI research roles concentrate in a few cities, like London.\nL71: ### 8. Adopt or modernize value-added taxes\nL72: \nL73: Six out of the G7 countries have national value-added taxes (VATs), as do 37 out of 38 OECD countries. The United States is the exception.\nL74: As AI transforms the economy, labor's share of the production of value might decline significantly. A shift toward taxing consumption (as through a VAT) could become necessary to fund core government activities. VAT collection also provides governments with fine-grained information about the economic production network—which could be particularly valuable during this potential period of rapid technological and economic change.\nL75: \"Value-added taxes are non-distortionary and to an extent, self-enforcing,\" notes John Horton of MIT's Sloan School of Management, a member of our Economic Advisory Council.\nL76: ### 9. Implement new revenue structures to account for AI’s growing share of the economy\nL77: If AI is responsible for a large share of economic output (causing labor’s share to decline), governments might require new revenue streams to complement income tax. Another of David Gamage’s proposals is exploring a \"low-rate business wealth tax\" as a complement to income taxes. His reasoning: \"Income taxes face accounting manipulation; wealth taxes face asset valuation challenges. Using both makes the system harder to avoid\" for highly profitable enterprises.\nL78: Gamage analogizes this system to the fee structures that certain asset managers charge clients: \"the wealth tax functions as a management fee for providing legal infrastructure protecting accumulated capital, while the income tax serves as a performance fee for profits generated in state markets.\" This idea represents one way that governments might adapt to changes in the value of human labor, although we think there are many more ideas to be explored in this area.\nL79: ## Continuing the conversation\nL80: \nL81: Earlier this fall, Anthropic announced a $10 million commitment to scale up the Economic Futures Program. This investment will support rigorous empirical research on AI's economic impacts and policy ideas, as well as expand our Symposia series—beginning with an event in London this November, which follows our September event in DC.\nL82: None of the ideas outlined here represent definitive recommendations. They are starting points for deeper research, policy development, and public debate. The economic effects of AI remain uncertain in both timing and magnitude, and different scenarios will require different responses.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2783view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research.google/pubs/?year=2025&q=language%20model\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQwen (https://qwen.ai/blog)\nciteturn2783view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://qwen.ai/blog\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCoral NPU: A full-stack platform for Edge AI (https://research.google/blog/coral-npu-a-full-stack-platform-for-edge-ai/)\nciteturn2783view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2779view4\",\"id\":113}); Total lines: 186\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://agent.minimax.io/docs/llms.txt)\nciteturn2783view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2777view4\",\"id\":0}); Total lines: 50\n"
